\title{bayesammi: Bayesian Estimation of the Additive Main Effects and Multiplicative Interaction Model}
\description{
\pkg{bayesammi} estimates the additive main effects and multiplicative interaction (AMMI) model of
multi-environment trials in plant breeding by Bayesian methods, with the Gibbs sampler of Crossa et al. (2011).
Every parameter has a posterior distribution, so the AMMI biplot gets statistical inference: highest posterior
density (HPD) regions show which genotypes and environments interact significantly and which form homogeneous
groups. Beyond the article, the package fits unbalanced trials, environment-specific error variances and the GGE
(sites regression) model, turns the posterior into decisions (winners, probabilities of superior performance and
stability, ranks, stability measures, predictions), compares and checks models, and runs every analysis in a
built-in application. It also fits the Finlay-Wilkinson, Eberhart-Russell and Shukla stability models by Gibbs
sampling and gives posterior distributions of classical and AMMI-based stability indices. The package is for breeders and agronomists who analyse genotype-by-environment interaction
and want uncertainty statements about their conclusions.
}
\details{
\strong{Functions}, in the order of an analysis:
\itemize{
  \item \code{\link{ge_mean}}, \code{\link{ge_var}}, \code{\link{g_eff}}, \code{\link{e_eff}},
    \code{\link{ge_eff}}: cell means and variances, main effects, interaction effects and their singular value
    decomposition.
  \item \code{\link{ge_ammi}}, \code{\link{ge_model}}: least-squares AMMI and the mixed model of the error variance.
  \item \code{\link{bam_priors}}, \code{\link{bayes_ammi}}: priors and the Gibbs sampler of the AMMI or GGE model,
    with a common or environment-specific error variances and missing plots or cells (chains, burn-in, thinning,
    posterior summaries with HPD intervals, convergence diagnostics, DIC and WAIC).
  \item \code{\link{biplots}}: the biplot with HPD regions.
  \item \code{\link{bam_predict}}, \code{\link{bam_winners}}, \code{\link{bam_superiority}},
    \code{\link{bam_ranks}}, \code{\link{bam_stability}}: decisions for breeders.
  \item \code{\link{bam_compare}}, \code{\link{bam_ppc}}, \code{\link{bam-draws}}: model choice, posterior
    predictive checks, and the draws for \pkg{posterior}, \pkg{bayesplot} and \pkg{coda}.
  \item \code{\link{bam_stab_priors}}, \code{\link{bayes_stability}}: Bayesian Finlay-Wilkinson,
    Eberhart-Russell and Shukla stability models; \code{\link{bam_indices}}, \code{\link{bam_ammi_indices}}:
    posterior distributions of classical and AMMI-based stability indices.
  \item \code{\link{bam_cite}}: the references of the methods behind a result (also printed under every result).
  \item \code{\link{bam_app}}: the built-in application.
  \item \code{\link{bam_interpret}}: a plain-language interpretation of a result.
  \item \code{\link{matrix_k}}, \code{\link{orthnorm}}: orthonormal contrasts and Gram-Schmidt orthonormalisation.
}
Every analysis returns the named list documented on its help page, with a class and methods (\code{print},
\code{summary}, \code{plot}, \code{as.data.table}); see \code{\link{bam-methods}}. Utilities:
\code{\link{bam_theme}}, \code{\link{bam_save}}, \code{\link{bam_notation}}, \code{\link{bam_reference}}.

\strong{Data.} \code{\link{Maiz}}: grain yield of 9 maize genotypes in 20 sites with 4 replicates (the example
of Crossa et al. 2011).

\strong{Vignettes.} Start with \code{vignette("bayesammi_01_getting_started", package = "bayesammi")};
\code{browseVignettes("bayesammi")} lists all of them, each as HTML and as PDF.
}
